## Solution

**Setup.** $\mathrm{PGL}(2,\mathbb{F}_{31}) = \mathrm{GL}(2,\mathbb{F}_{31})/\mathbb{F}_{31}^{\times}$, where we quotient by scalar matrices. The order of a class $[A]$ is the smallest $n$ with $A^n$ scalar.

**Classification.** For $A\in\mathrm{GL}(2,\mathbb{F}_{31})$, the minimal polynomial has degree $\le 2$. Over $\overline{\mathbb{F}_{31}}$ either $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues (semisimple) or a repeated eigenvalue (unipotent, after scaling). This gives three cases.

**Case 1 — Split semisimple.** $A$ has distinct eigenvalues $\lambda,\mu\in\mathbb{F}_{31}^{\times}$. Over $\mathbb{F}_{31}$, $A$ is conjugate to $\mathrm{diag}(\lambda,\mu)$, so in $\mathrm{PGL}$ it is conjugate to $\mathrm{diag}(\lambda/\mu,1)$. Its order equals $\mathrm{ord}(\lambda/\mu)$ in $\mathbb{F}_{31}^{\times}$, which divides $|\mathbb{F}_{31}^{\times}|=30$. **Maximum: $30$.**

**Case 2 — Non-split semisimple.** The eigenvalues lie in $\mathbb{F}_{31^2}\setminus\mathbb{F}_{31}$, of the form $\alpha,\alpha^{31}$ with $\alpha\in\mathbb{F}_{31^2}^{\times}$. Over $\mathbb{F}_{31^2}$, $A$ is conjugate to $\mathrm{diag}(\alpha,\alpha^{31})$. Then $A^n$ is scalar iff $\alpha^n=(\alpha^{31})^n$, i.e. $\alpha^{30n}=1$, i.e. $\alpha^n\in\mathbb{F}_{31}^{\times}$. Thus the order of $[A]$ is the order of $\alpha$ in the quotient
$$\mathbb{F}_{31^2}^{\times}/\mathbb{F}_{31}^{\times},$$
which is cyclic of order $\frac{31^2-1}{31-1}=32$. Hence the order divides $32$, and taking $\alpha$ a generator of $\mathbb{F}_{31^2}^{\times}$ (order $960$) gives an image of order $960/\gcd(960,30)=960/30=32$. Concretely, viewing $\mathbb{F}_{31^2}$ as a $2$-dimensional $\mathbb{F}_{31}$-vector space, multiplication by such an $\alpha$ is an $\mathbb{F}_{31}$-linear map whose class in $\mathrm{PGL}(2,\mathbb{F}_{31})$ has order $32$. **Maximum: $32$, and it is attained.**

**Case 3 — Unipotent.** After scaling, $A$ is conjugate to $\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$, whose $n$-th power is $\begin{pmatrix}1&n\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$, scalar only when $n\equiv 0\pmod{31}$. **Order: $31$.**

**Conclusion.** Every element of $\mathrm{PGL}(2,\mathbb{F}_{31})$ has order dividing $30$ (split), dividing $32$ (non-split), or equal to $31$ (unipotent). The largest among $\{30,32,31\}$ is $32$, and it is realized by a generator of the non-split torus.

$$\boxed{32}$$

